The Problem: Right now, when you click "System Oversight," you are immediately dropped into the "New Case" form. It feels a bit like a data-entry tool rather than a Director's dashboard.
The Solution: Create a "Command Center" or "Analytics" tab as the default landing page for the Admin Dashboard. We can fetch and display high-level metrics: Total Registered Agents, Active Investigation Rooms, and Win/Loss ratios for each case to see if a case is too hard or too easy.

3. Smart Filtering & Collapsible Lists
The Problem: As your game grows, a case might have 50 pieces of evidence and 15 suspects. When you select that case in the Admin dropdown, the "Manage Existing..." list at the bottom of the page is going to become a massive, endlessly scrolling wall of text.
The Solution: Upgrade the "Manage Existing" UI. We can add quick text-search filters (e.g., typing "gun" instantly filters the evidence list) and make the parent categories (like Phases) collapsible accordions so the author can keep their workspace clean.

4. In the context of game development, this falls under the category of a business logic vulnerability or a gameplay integrity issue. It isn't a critical infrastructure security flaw—meaning a hacker can't use this URL to take over your server or steal user passwords—but it absolutely breaks the core rules and tension of your game.

If the "one chance to listen" mechanic is crucial to the tension of the wiretap phase, then yes, exposing the raw URL is a flaw that needs to be patched.

Fortunately, you can resolve this completely by moving the audio files behind Laravel's authorization layer, rather than serving them directly from a public directory.

Here are the two best ways to secure those files.

Option 1: The Authenticated Streaming Route (Recommended)
Instead of putting the .wav or .mp3 files in your public folder, you move them to a private storage disk (like storage/app/private) and serve them through a dedicated Laravel API route.

This allows you to leverage your existing database locks to authorize the request before sending a single byte of audio.

How it works:

Relocate the Files: Store your wiretap audio files in a non-public directory.

Create a Gatekeeper Endpoint: Set up a route like GET /api/rooms/{room}/questions/{question}/audio.

Check the Locks: In your controller, before returning the file, you run three checks:

Is the user authenticated?

Are they a participant in this specific room?

Crucially: Has this question_id already been recorded in the room_played_wiretaps table?

Serve or Deny: If the wiretap has already been burned, you return a 403 Forbidden. If it's valid, you use Laravel's response()->file() or response()->stream() to send the audio to the frontend.

Even if a player copies the API URL and pastes it into their browser later, the server will see that the wiretap is marked as "played" in the database and block the request.

Phase 1: The Data Layer (Centralizing API Communication)Currently, every form manually declares createMutation, updateMutation, and deleteMutation, alongside a redundant useQuery fetch for the cases. This is the equivalent of writing raw SQL queries inside your UI views.  The Goal: Move all network communication into dedicated custom hooks.The Files to Create/Modify:src/hooks/useAdminData.ts (Smart): A centralized hook that wraps useQuery for fetching and caching fetchAdminCases. It will distribute the data to the rest of the app, meaning the network request only happens once.src/hooks/useAdminMutations.ts (Smart): A generic factory hook. Instead of rewriting mutations, you will pass an entity type (e.g., 'case', 'evidence', 'question'). It will return standardized create, update, and delete functions with built-in error handling and toast notifications.Phase 2: The Context Layer (Lifting Global State)In the current setup, every builder form (AdminStandardBuilder, AdminLocationBuilder, etc.) manages its own local state for caseId, phaseId, and levelId. If an admin switches from the Evidence tab to the Interrogation tab, they have to re-select everything from cascading dropdowns.  The Goal: Create a single source of truth for what the admin is currently looking at.The Files to Create/Modify:src/context/AdminContext.tsx (Smart): This file will house the global state. It will store selectedCaseId, selectedPhaseId, and selectedLevelId. It will consume useAdminData to provide the derived lists (e.g., availablePhases, availableLevels) directly to any component that needs them.src/pages/Admin/AdminDashboard.tsx (Smart): This becomes the master layout. It will wrap the forms in the AdminProvider and house a left-hand sidebar where the global Case/Phase/Level selections are made.Phase 3: The Utility Layer (Standardizing Serialization)Your AdminStandardBuilder, AdminLocationBuilder, and AdminWiretapBuilder all contain massive, duplicated blocks of FormData appending logic, ignoring the appendChoicesToFormData utility you already started building in questionUtils.ts.  The Goal: Eliminate all FormData manipulation inside the React components.The Files to Create/Modify:src/pages/Admin/forms/Shared/questionUtils.ts (Dumb/Utility): Expand this file to include comprehensive serializers. Create functions like serializeStandardNode(state), serializeLocationNode(state), and serializeInterceptNode(state). The React component should just pass its state object to this utility, and the utility hands back a perfectly formatted FormData object ready for the API.Phase 4: The Presentation Layer (Smart vs. Dumb Components)Right now, your form files are monolithic. They handle network requests, state management, complex derived logic, and rendering thousands of lines of JSX.  The Goal: Split the UI into Container (Smart) components and Presentational (Dumb) components.The Files to Create/Modify (Example: Standard Builder):AdminStandardBuilder.tsx (Smart Container): Its only job is to consume AdminContext to get the selected Level, initialize local form state (text, choices), format the data using the Utility Layer, and trigger the save function from useAdminMutations. It handles no complex UI rendering.AdminStandardForm.tsx (Dumb Component): This component takes props (text, setText, choices, handleSave, isProcessing). It has zero knowledge of APIs, Contexts, or network requests. It simply renders the inputs and triggers the callback functions passed to it.ChoiceEditorCard.tsx (Dumb Component): You already have this started. Ensure it remains completely decoupled from network logic, acting only as a UI visualizer for a choice object.  By executing this plan, your form components will shrink by 60%, your API logic will be fixed in exactly one place, and your UI will be fully decoupled from your data architecture.

1. The GameRoom.tsx Monolith (Controller Anti-Pattern)Currently, GameRoom.tsx acts as a massive "God Component." It manages route parameters, fetches room data, calculates derived unread notification states, establishes real-time Laravel Echo socket listeners, handles clipboard interactions, controls the victory/defeat overlays, and acts as the layout wrapper for the sidebar and tabs.  The Flaw: When a component has this many responsibilities, any minor state change (like copying an invite code) risks re-rendering heavy sub-trees unnecessarily.The Fix: We need to split GameRoom.tsx into smaller, focused layers. The top level should only handle data fetching and providing context. The layout (Sidebar, Main Workspace) should be extracted into a Presentational component. The Victory/Defeat overlay should be its own isolated component that simply listens to the Context.2. Side-Effects in Data Fetching (useGameRoom.ts)Inside useGameRoom.ts, the queryFn is mutating sessionStorage (setting the active_room_id_for_${inviteCode}).  The Flaw: React Query assumes queryFn is a pure function. If a query is retried due to a network blip or window refocus, it will re-execute that storage mutation. Side-effects should not live inside your data-fetching logic.The Fix: Move the sessionStorage logic out of the queryFn. It should be handled in a useEffect that listens for a successful data fetch, or managed within the API service layer before React Query even touches the data.3. Prop Drilling in the Campaign HierarchyLook at CampaignTab.tsx and how it renders LocationPhase or WiretapPhase. It passes down level, status, localVotes, handleSelectChoice, currentUserId, addToast, room, and accumulatedEvidences.  The Flaw: This is classic prop drilling. You are pulling state from useRoomState at the top of the tab, only to pass it through multiple layers of components that don't need it, just so the child components can access it.The Fix: Since you already built an excellent RoomProvider, the deeply nested components (LocationPhase, WiretapPhase, etc.) should consume useRoomState directly. CampaignTab should only pass down the specific level data it maps over, instantly cleaning up the component signatures.4. Real-Time Socket Listener DuplicationIn useInvestigationPhase.ts, you establish Echo.private(room.${roomId}) listeners inside a useEffect.  The Flaw: Because this is a custom hook, every component that calls useInvestigationPhase will mount its own instance of these socket listeners. While Laravel Echo handles channel sharing decently, setting up multiple identical listeners across the component tree can lead to race conditions, duplicated toast notifications (like multiple "WIRETAP INTERCEPTED" popups), and memory leaks.The Fix: Real-time listeners that affect global room state should be lifted into the RoomProvider or a dedicated useRoomSockets hook at the highest level of the room. The event payloads can then simply update the Context, which naturally flows down to the rest of the app.


